// A nexus -- `@name`, `::name` or `::name` -- needs a primary group: `(args)`,
// `[content]` or a `|` run. `{value}`, `#(id)` and a `::connect` only add to
// one of them and cannot stand alone. Every line here is a valid nexus; the
// ones that are not live in `nexus-invalid-*.tmt`, one to a file because the
// parser stops at the first error.

=[ Primary groups ]

@memo()

@memo[ 本文 ]

@memo(a: 1)

@memo(a: 1)[ 本文 ]

@memo(a)[ 本文 ]#(first)

=[ Secondary groups follow a primary one ]

@memo(){ k: v }

@memo()#(second)

@memo(a: 1){ k: v }#(third)

@memo(){ k: v }#(fourth)::rule(allow: (card))

=[ Connects ]

A nameless connect `::{...}` or `::(...)` is the one place `{...}` stands without
a nexus of its own: it puts data into the host it follows.

@memo(a: 1)::{ b: 2 }

@memo()::(b: 2)

@memo(a: 1)::rule(allow: (card))::{ b: 2 }

- 項目 ::{ k: v }

- 項目 ::(k: v)

- 項目 ::rule(allow: (card))

= 見出し ::{ k: v }

= 見出し ::rule(allow: (card))

=[ In running text ]

An `@` with no group after it is prose: me@example.com, or a mention of @someone.
An empty element is written with its group: a @memo() in a sentence, or a
@memo[ body ] with content.

=[ In a list ]

-@memo(a: 1)
-@memo[ 本文 ]
-@memo()
